# The Yard — what never goes to GitHub.
# Keep the repository PRIVATE: it still holds the escape room's solution
# (private/, ESCAPE_ROOM_FLOW.md, context/) and attendees' Telegram usernames
# in the tests. See DEPLOY.md.

# Secrets: bot token, Flask key, password and PIN hashes.
.env

# The live database, its backups, and anything with attendees' personal
# details (names, emails, payment screenshots, Paperform exports).
data/
imports/
private/receipts/
context/The_Yard_Sign_Up_Responses.xlsx
*.xlsx

# Built from the sign-up list by scripts/build_design_file.py, so they carry
# real names. Rebuild them locally instead.
design/answers.json
design/the-yard-design.html
design/index.html.bak

# Stays on the laptop, by the organiser's decision (23 Sep). It is a review of
# the escape room that names the killer, the code and every hint, and the
# repository is public. ESCAPE_ROOM_FLOW.md is already up there and is worse,
# which is an argument for making the repository private, not for adding to it.
ESCAPE_ROOM_PROPOSAL.md

# The generated camera stills and bin photographs (EVIDENCE_BRIEF.md section 7).
# These are printed at the desk and are never served, so Render has no use for
# them, and they are the clearest possible spoiler: still-5 shows the killer and
# the oven clock that breaks the case. Large files, too. The folder's README is
# committed so a new agent knows what belongs there; the images are not.
# See private/props/README.md.
private/props/*
!private/props/README.md

# Python and tooling.
venv/
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
*.log

# Editors and the OS.
.vscode/
.idea/
Thumbs.db
.DS_Store

# Empty leftover folders from the first setup.
my-mini-app/
